WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Friday,

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.200
the sixth of November. This
episode is written with AI,

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.760
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.613
Crude bounced about four
percent on Thursday.

5
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:15.920
Traders doubted that the midterm
result means a quick end to the Iran

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.160
war, and Tehran says it is exempting
China and India from its restrictions

7
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.400
in the Strait of Hormuz. In the US,
refiners are making less gasoline

8
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.613
than in any recent year so they can
turn out more diesel and jet fuel.

9
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:32.560
On the markets. Brent was up four
percent at about ninety-eight dollars

10
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.720
forty a barrel by mid-afternoon
in New York on Thursday,

11
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.800
according to OilPrice dot com.

12
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.280
West Texas Intermediate was up nearly
three percent at ninety dollars

13
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.600
seventy. Front-month European gas
at the Dutch Title Transfer Facility

14
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.693
closed at eighty-nine euros
seventy a megawatt hour,

15
00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:52.040
while Henry Hub was flat at
just under three dollars.

16
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.840
In storage, EU gas sites were
seventy-three point eight percent full

17
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.640
on the fourth of November, according
to Gas Infrastructure Europe.

18
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.707
Starting with crude. Thursday was
the first gain in four sessions,

19
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:07.080
and it followed a Wednesday settle
of ninety-four dollars sixty.

20
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.320
The selling carried on
into Thursday morning,

21
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.520
with Brent at ninety-four dollars
thirty before Europe opened,

22
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.120
and then turned around.

23
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.600
According to OilPrice dot com and Rigzone,

24
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.240
traders were rethinking whether a
Democratic Congress can actually shorten

25
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.520
the war while Trump stays in office.

26
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.600
European gas took the same route.

27
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.640
It was down about two percent early
on hopes that Qatari liquefied natural

28
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.080
gas would come back through Hormuz,
then finished nearly two percent

29
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.020
higher.

30
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.787
None of this reflects a
change in physical flows.

31
00:01:35.813 --> 00:01:38.373
The risk premium is trading
on political signals,

32
00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:42.560
and that matters to Gulf exporters,
Asian buyers and Europe's gas importers

33
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.880
alike. The reports we've seen today
contain no ceasefire terms and no

34
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.520
schedule for talks.

35
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.560
Staying in the Gulf. Iran's parliament
says it is exempting several key

36
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.320
trading partners, China
and India among them,

37
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.960
from its oil transit
restrictions in Hormuz.

38
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.360
Ahmad Hayat-Moghaddam, vice chair of
parliament's national security committee,

39
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.720
announced it on Tuesday,
according to OilPrice dot com.

40
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.000
The US naval blockade of
Iranian ports continues.

41
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.120
This is Iran's own announcement.

42
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.027
No vessel movements under the
exemption have been reported,

43
00:02:14.053 --> 00:02:16.560
and neither Beijing nor
New Delhi has commented.

44
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.800
If it does hold, Chinese and Indian
refiners could win back some Gulf

45
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.120
barrels, while other buyers stay
squeezed on freight and insurance.

46
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.680
Also on Tuesday, Iran's Islamic
Revolutionary Guard Corps said two oil

47
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.040
tankers and a tug caught
fire in the strait.

48
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.480
By its account, one tanker hit
a mine while heading south,

49
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.840
and the tug caught fire after
going to help the second.

50
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.480
Only Iran has reported these incidents.

51
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.720
There is no confirmation yet from
owners, flag states or the United Kingdom

52
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.040
Maritime Trade Operations centre.

53
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.240
Over to US refining. According to the EIA,

54
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.560
gasoline made up less than forty-five
percent of US refinery output from

55
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.720
January through August. That was
below the previous five-year low in

56
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.480
every one of those months.

57
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.000
The usual range runs from
forty-five to fifty percent.

58
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.920
The EIA puts the shift down to
unusually strong margins on distillates

59
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.680
and jet fuel, using monthly
supply data through August.

60
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.720
Refiners are running hard
while they make the switch.

61
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.400
Utilisation was ninety-four point five
percent in the week to the thirtieth

62
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.320
of October, up nearly two points.

63
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.800
Distillate stocks that week stood at
about ninety-nine point three million

64
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.120
barrels. More middle distillates
from Gulf Coast plants eases the US

65
00:03:31.227 --> 00:03:34.480
diesel balance, and leaves
gasoline relatively short.

66
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.480
Diesel is tightening on the
other side of the Pacific.

67
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.040
Chinese refiners have sold about five
hundred thousand tonnes of diesel

68
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.320
for November loading so
far, Reuters reported.

69
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.760
Exports are set to fall from
their August and September highs,

70
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.547
because those late-summer shipments
ran domestic inventories down.

71
00:03:51.573 --> 00:03:55.520
Deals for loading in the second half
of November aren't finalised yet.

72
00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.707
Less Chinese diesel in Asia supports
regional middle-distillate cracks.

73
00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:03.013
It could also pull Middle
Eastern and Indian cargoes east,

74
00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:04.680
which leaves less for Europe.

75
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.880
Turning to European gas. Storage was
seventy-four point one percent on

76
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.680
the third of November, and
seventy-three point eight on the fourth.

77
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.120
That's a drop of about a third
of a point in a single day.

78
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.800
Unnamed EU diplomats told EUobserver
the bloc has no plans to resume

79
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.880
buying Russian gas.
They spoke ahead of US,

80
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.080
Ukraine and Russia talks
in Abu Dhabi next week.

81
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.000
That keeps Europe's winter balance
resting on liquefied natural gas.

82
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.987
On the water, Rhine levels are forecast
to fall again in the coming days,

83
00:04:35.093 --> 00:04:39.200
